Published **2026-10-21**.

# Two build steps

The website and the generator are separate build products. **Zine** parses
the SuperMD content and renders shared SuperHTML layouts, navigation, and assets.
A native Zig coordinator validates the complete release. **Zig 0.17.0** compiles
the generator and the sibling zast library for `wasm32-freestanding`. The final
site contains HTML, CSS, JavaScript, artwork, and the compiled `.wasm` asset.

The site source lives outside the library. Documentation, posts, and the browser
integration can evolve without becoming part of the library's source tree.

# A small browser boundary

JavaScript loads the precompiled module and copies the input JSON into its
memory. The Zig bridge validates the enum description, builds `zast.Field`
values, emits a `zast.EnumDeclaration` into an `AstContext`, and calls
`finish()` followed by `renderAlloc()`.

The browser receives formatted source and inspected AST data. The output views
show the Zig source, equivalent builder code, actual node tags, and actual token
spellings. Input edits invoke the same local module; the host serves static
assets and does not generate code.

# Descriptions are the editable input

JSON is a small runtime description of the constructs the compiled generator
supports. The equivalent builder view makes that description's connection to
the Zig API visible. It is explanatory output, not an editor backed by a Zig
compiler.

The first example supports inferred and explicit enum backing types, signed
values, empty enums, and non-exhaustive tags. Arbitrary edited Zig compilation,
struct generation, and function generation are not part of this browser bridge.
Those library components can be exposed by future examples with their own input
contracts.

# Keep the feedback loop inspectable

Invalid input displays a diagnostic while preserving the last successful output.
Copy and download operate locally. Shared URLs intentionally encode the selected
input; the example has no generation API, analytics, or external editor service.

[Open the enum example](/examples/) or
[build your first declaration locally](/docs/getting-started/).

